L000256	{\it Introduction.}--
L000257	Understanding the behavior of mesons within the nuclear medium offers vital insights into the non-perturbative aspects of Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) \cite{REVIEW01,HAYANO2010,REVIEW02}.
L000258	In particular, the low-lying pseudoscalar nonet provides information deeply connected to the underlying symmetries of QCD.
L000259	Under $U(3)_L \times U(3)_R$ chiral symmetry that can be organized into $SU(3)_V \times SU(3)_A \times U(1)_V\times U(1)_A$, the spontaneous breaking of $SU(3)_A$ symmetry
L000260	gives rise to the octet pseudoscalar Nambu-Goldstone (NG) bosons (3$\pi$'s, 4$K$'s, and $\eta$) \cite{NAMBU1960-2,NAMBU1960,NJL}.
L000261	In contrast, the  $U(1)_A$ symmetry plays a crucial role in shaping the mass spectrum, especially of the $\eta'(958)$ meson that is known as a combination of the singlet $\eta$ ($\eta_0$) as a main component and the octet $\eta$ ($\eta_8$).
L000262	The $\eta_0$ cannot serve as an NG boson due to the presence of the axial $U(1)$ anomaly.
L000263	The $\eta'$ meson, therefore, has a significantly larger mass compared with the mass of the NG bosons in vacuum \cite{WEINBERG1975,WITTEN1979,VENEZIANO1979}.
L000264	
L000265	The interior of a nucleus exhibits a highly dense environment of approximately $2\times 10^{14}$~$\mathrm{g/cm^3}$, resembling the universe shortly after the Big Bang, where chiral symmetry may partially be restored \cite{HATSUDALEE1992,HAYANO2010}.
L000266	It is anticipated that the mass of the $\eta'$ meson ($\sim\eta_0$) decreases, similar to a typical $q\bar{q}$ meson in the nuclear medium.
L000267	Simultaneously, the axial $U(1)$ anomaly may also be less significant if the in-medium $\eta^\prime$ mass reduction happens.
L000268	In this context, theoretical calculations that consider both spontaneous breaking of chiral symmetry and axial $U(1)$ anomaly predict the in-medium $\eta'$ mass deviation $\Delta m_{\eta'}$ of approximately $-150$ \cite{COSTA2003.171,COSTA2005.71,PhysRevC.74.045203} and $-(80$--$100)$ \cite{PhysRevC.88.064906} $\mathrm{MeV/c^2}$ at the normal nuclear density, based on the Nambu--Jona-Lasinio (NJL) and linear $\sigma$ models, respectively.
L000269	Note that there is another prediction by the Quark Meson Coupling (QMC) model \cite{SAITO20071}, giving $\Delta m_{\eta'}\sim -60$~$\mathrm{MeV/c^2}$ \cite{BASS2006368} according to the mixing angle of $\eta_0$ and $\eta_8$.
L000270	
L000271	However, it remains an open question whether the suppression of anomaly effects accompanies the restoration of chiral symmetry, although the interplay between them is being suggested theoretically \cite{10.1093/ptep/ptab084,LEE1996,JIDO2012}.
L000272	Examining the spectral function of $\eta'$ in the nuclear medium opens a new avenue for understanding the impacts of chiral symmetry restoration and the axial $U(1)$ anomaly under dense conditions.
L000273	For this purpose, the present work aims to measure the invariant mass of $\eta'$ mesons within a nucleus through the $\eta'\to\gamma\gamma$ channel.
L000274	This channel has an advantage because the $\gamma\gamma$ invariant mass directly reflects the in-medium $\eta'$ mass,
L000275	giving clearer signals with no final state interactions.
L000276	There should also be no influence on the two $\gamma$'s four-momenta by energy loss.
L000277	
L000278	
L000279	{\it Previous experimental studies.}--
L000280	Although no evidence for an in-medium modification of the $\eta^{\prime}$ mass spectrum has been reported to date, several experimental studies have investigated the $\eta^{\prime}$ mass in nuclei using indirect methods.
L000281	The CBELSA/TAPS collaboration measured the depth of the $\eta^{\prime}$–nucleus potential, $V_{0}\sim -40~\mathrm{MeV}$ \cite{NANOVA2013,NANOVA2016,NANOVA2018}, which corresponds to the mass deviation at normal nuclear density.
L000282	The COSY-11 collaboration extracted the scattering length of $\etap p$ interaction \cite{PhysRevLett.113.062004}.
L000283	They claimed that the value is consistent with the prediction by the QMC model, while it disfavors the expectation by the NJL and linear $\sigma$ models.
L000284	Furthermore, the $\eta$-PRiME/Super-FRS collaboration \cite{YKTANAKA2018} and the LEPS2/BGOegg collaboration \cite{TOMIDA2020} searched for $\eta^{\prime}$-nucleus bound states, which could be formed if the $\eta^{\prime}$–nucleus system offers a deeply attractive potential with small absorption.
L000285	Their studies have not revealed any bound states, indicating that a large mass reduction exceeding $100~\mathrm{MeV/c^2}$ is disfavored.
L000286	
L000287	On the other hand, an analysis using the RHIC data of Au+Au collisions has claimed an $\etap$ mass reduction larger than $200~\mathrm{MeV/c^2}$ based on the two-pion Bose-Einstein correlation functions~\cite{HIC2010}.
L000288	However, a similar analysis using the CERN SPS data has indicated no significant mass modification~\cite{HIC2011}.
L000289	
L000290	
L000291	{\it Experiment.}--
L000292	The experiment was conducted using a 20 mm thick carbon target on the SPring-8 LEPS2 beamline \cite{MURAMATSU2014184}, which provided a tagged photon beam with the intensity of a few times $10^6$/s in the energy range of $1.3$--$2.4$~$\mathrm{GeV}$.
L000293	This beam was produced by laser Compton scattering,
L000294	being clean with small low-energy components.
L000295	A high-resolution electromagnetic calorimeter, BGOegg \cite{ISHIKAWA2016109}, was used to measure the energies and angles of two photons.
L000296	The precise measurement of them is crucial to find any anomalous shape in the $\gamma\gamma$ invariant mass spectrum.
L000297	The BGOegg calorimeter is an egg-shaped assembly of 1320 BGO crystals, covering the polar angles from 24$^\circ$ to 144$^\circ$ with a good segmentation of $4$--$6^\circ$ in both polar and azimuthal directions.
L000298	Each crystal has a sufficient thickness of 220 mm, corresponding to 20 radiation lengths.
L000299	It features a self-supporting structure that eliminates the insensitive area between adjacent crystals.
L000300	This design enables BGOegg to achieve an energy resolution of 1.38\% for 1 GeV photons, which is among the highest globally.
L000301	A charged particle was also detected by this calorimeter, together with a hit signal at the corresponding inner plastic scintillator.
L000302	In addition, a planar drift chamber covered polar angles less than $22^\circ$ for the detection of charged particles.
L000303	Experimental details are given in Refs. \cite{PhysRevC.106.035201,PhysRevC.100.055202,ISHIKAWA2016109}.
L000304	
L000305	
L000306	{\it Analysis.}--
L000307	The $\eta^{\prime}\to\gamma\gamma$ events were selected under the condition that exactly two photons were detected by the BGOegg calorimeter.
L000308	The invariant mass spectrum was reconstructed from the four-momenta of these photons.
L000309	At most one charged particle was allowed in the final state to select the quasi-free reactions $\gamma N\to\eta^{\prime}N$, where $N$ denotes a proton or a neutron. No further constraints on the recoiling proton or neutron were imposed in the analysis.
L000310	Additional criteria on the electromagnetic shower shape and hit timing of the detected $\gamma$'s were required to reduce background events arising from the misidentification of neutrons, cosmic rays, and particles produced upstream of the detector setup.
L000311	The $\gamma$ selection criteria on the electromagnetic shower shape were optimized using a Monte Carlo (MC) simulation based on GEANT4 \cite{GEANT4}.
L000312	These criteria have been confirmed not to introduce a bias in the $\gamma\gamma$ invariant mass distribution.
L000313	
L000314	The energy calibration of the BGOegg calorimeter was performed using the $\pi^{0}$ and $\eta$ mass peaks in the $\gamma\gamma$ invariant mass distributions.
L000315	Energy leakage from a $\gamma$ cluster reconstructed by connecting hit crystals was evaluated for its correction as a function of energy and hit position using the MC simulation.
L000316	The reconstructed meson masses were confirmed to be independent of momentum and emission angle over a wide range, validating the quality of the calibration.
L000317	
